- How do you handle duplicates and negative values?

## Why This Is Asked
Real-time analytics require fast percentile queries with inserts/deletes while handling duplicates.

## Key Concepts
- Coordinate compression maps latencies to a compact domain.
- Fenwick tree maintains prefix counts efficiently.
- kth-order statistic via binary lifting yields the percentile value.
- Carefully handle erase when a value count reaches zero.

## Code Example
```javascript
class Fenwick {
  constructor(n){ this.n=n; this.bit=new Array(n+1).fill(0); }
  update(i, delta){ for(; i<=this.n; i+= i&-i) this.bit[i]+=delta; }
  query(i){ let s=0; for(; i>0; i-= i&-i) s+=this.bit[i]; return s; }
  kth(k){ let idx=0; let bit=1; while((bit<<1) <= this.n) bit<<=1; for(; bit>0; bit>>=1){ let nxt = idx + bit; if(nxt <= this.n && this.bit[nxt] < k){ idx = nxt; k -= this.bit[nxt]; } } return idx+1; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle unseen latency values without rebuilding the compression map?
- Compare this approach to a wavelet tree for large domains or streaming data.

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests mastery of persistent data structures, real-world versioning systems, and memory-time trade-offs. It also probes understanding of shared nodes and functional update semantics.

## Key Concepts
- Fully persistent data structures via path-copying
- Implicit treap or segment tree with augmented sums and sizes
- Version management with O(log n) mutations per update
- Correct boundary handling for empty intervals and edge operations

## Code Example
```javascript
class Node {
  constructor(val, prio, left = null, right = null) {
    this.val = val;
    this.prio = prio;
    this.left = left;
    this.right = right;
    this.size = 1;
    this.sum = val;
  }

  update() {
    this.size = 1 + (this.left?.size ?? 0) + (this.right?.size ?? 0);
    this.sum = this.val + (this.left?.sum ?? 0) + (this.right?.sum ?? 0);
  }
}

function split(root, k) {
  if (!root) return [null, null];
  const leftSize = root.left?.size ?? 0;
  if (k <= leftSize) {
    const [l, r] = split(root.left, k);
    root.left = r;
    root.update();
    return [l, root];
  } else {
    const [l, r] = split(root.right, k - leftSize - 1);
    root.right = l;
    root.update();
    return [root, r];
  }
}

function merge(left, right) {
  if (!left || !right) return left ?? right;
  if (left.prio < right.prio) {
    left.right = merge(left.right, right);
    left.update();
    return left;
  } else {
    right.left = merge(left, right.left);
    right.update();
    return right;
  }
}

function rangeSum(root, l, r) {
  const [, mid] = split(root, l);
  const [target, rest] = split(mid, r - l + 1);
  const result = target?.sum ?? 0;
  return merge(merge(null, target), rest);
}

function insertAt(root, index, val) {
  const [left, right] = split(root, index);
  const newNode = new Node(val, Math.random());
  return merge(merge(left, newNode), right);
}

function eraseAt(root, index) {
  const [left, mid] = split(root, index);
  const [, right] = split(mid, 1);
  return merge(left, right);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a prefix-based weighted index with duplicates and per-prefix aggregates. Uses a trie with per-node weight distributions to support fast sum and kth-largest queries by prefix.

## Key Concepts

- Trie over strings and per-node multisets of weights
- Handling duplicates via multiset entries
- Order-statistics to extract kth-largest with duplicates
- Trade-offs: update cost proportional to word length, query cost to O(log M)

## Code Example

```javascript
class TrieNode { constructor(){ this.next=new Map(); this.weights=new Multiset(); } }
class WeightedTrie { constructor(){ this.root=new TrieNode(); }
  insertWord(word,w){ let node=this.root; for(const ch of word){ if(!node.next.has(ch)) node.next.set(ch, new TrieNode()); node=node.next.get(ch); node.weights.add(w); } }
  eraseWord(word,w){ let node=this.root; for(const ch of word){ if(!node.next.has(ch)) return; node=node.next.get(ch); node.weights.deleteOne(w); } }
  sumWeightsForPrefix(prefix){ let node=this.root; for(const ch of prefix){ if(!node.next.has(ch)) return 0; node=node.next.get(ch); } return node.weights.sum(); }
  kthLargestWeightForPrefix(prefix,k){ let node=this.root; for(const ch of prefix){ if(!node.next.has(ch)) return null; node=node.next.get(ch); } return node.weights.kthLargest(k); }
}
```

